<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dependency of Ireland on Great Britain Act 1719</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1720">1720</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Declaratory Act 1719</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dependency of Ireland on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> Act, known as the Declaratory Act, was passed by the British Parliament in 1720. It declared that the Parliament at Westminster had full power to make laws binding Ireland and removed the appellate jurisdiction of the Irish House of Lords. Its repeal in 1782, under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-grattan">Henry Grattan</a> and the Volunteers, began the period of Irish legislative independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The act grew out of a routine lawsuit. In 1709 the Irish Court of Exchequer heard a dispute between Maurice Annesley and his cousin Hester Sherlock over lands at Naas, County Kildare, and found for Annesley. The Irish House of Lords upheld Sherlock's appeal, whereupon Annesley turned to the British House of Lords, whose right to hear appeals from Irish courts had long been disputed, and it found for him. When the Barons of the Exchequer obeyed the British decree, the Irish Lords imprisoned them for contempt of parliament. The uproar was out of all proportion to the case, and it raised the old question of which house was the final court of appeal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>In the House of Commons the bill was opposed chiefly as serving only to increase the power of the House of Lords; critics also argued that its preamble and enacting section contradicted each other and that Ireland had historically had an independent judiciary. Supported by Joseph Jekyll and Philip Yorke, it was carried by 140 votes to 83.</p><p>Its first section declared the Kingdom of Ireland subordinate to and dependent upon the British crown, and the king, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-great-britain">Parliament of Great Britain</a>, to have &quot;full power and authority to make laws and statutes of sufficient validity to bind the Kingdom and people of Ireland&quot;. The second declared that the Irish House of Lords had no jurisdiction over judgements of Irish courts and made void all its proceedings on them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Irish House of Lords was infuriated, and the Barons of the Exchequer, though soon released, were fiercely vilified. Many thought the Irish Lords had brought the crisis on themselves, but the act, which its opponents in the Irish Patriot Party called the Sixth of George I after its regnal year, remained a grievance for decades and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poynings-law">Poynings' Law</a>, a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a>'s subservience.</p><p>It served as the model for the American Colonies Act 1766, also known as the Declaratory Act, which became a similar grievance in the Thirteen Colonies. Britain's defeat in the American War of Independence brought a more conciliatory tone towards Ireland, and the Repeal of Act for Securing Dependence of Ireland Act 1782 cleared the way for the Constitution of 1782.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Declaratory Act 1719” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ireland had its own Parliament of Lords and Commons, but Poynings’ Law of 1494 required its bills to be approved in advance by the English council, and the <b>Declaratory Act</b> of 1720 asserted the Westminster Parliament’s right to legislate for Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
